---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-699 — CLI quickstart docs. Covers the install, the V-266
// browser-OAuth-style activation flow, and the most common
// commands. Companion to https://docs.driftstack.dev/quickstart-curl/ (which targets
// direct-curl usage).
---

<BaseLayout title="CLI quickstart — Driftstack developer docs" description="Install the Driftstack CLI, authorize it from the browser, and run your first session.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>CLI quickstart</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      The Driftstack CLI is a thin wrapper around the REST API. It
      handles the API-key dance via a browser-OAuth-style flow so
      you don't paste keys into shell config; from there everything
      maps 1:1 to the underlying HTTP endpoints.
    </p>

    <h2>Install</h2>
    <pre><code>{`# macOS / Linux
brew install driftstack/tap/driftstack

# Or via npm globally
npm install -g @driftstack/cli

# Verify
driftstack --version
# → driftstack/2.3.x`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The CLI is a single binary (Node.js / Bun runtime). On Apple
      Silicon, the Homebrew tap ships native arm64 builds; x86_64
      Linux + Intel Mac get prebuilt binaries; everything else
      falls back to the npm package which runs under your local
      Node.
    </p>

 <h2>Authorize from the browser </h2>
    <p>
      The CLI doesn't ask you to paste an API key. Run:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`driftstack login`}</code></pre>
    <p>It will:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Mint a one-time activation code via
        <code>POST /v1/auth/cli-authorize/initiate</code>.</li>
      <li>Print a browser URL like
        <code>https://app.driftstack.dev/cli/authorize?code=…&amp;state=…</code>.</li>
      <li>Open the URL in your default browser (or you copy-paste it
        on a headless machine).</li>
      <li>You sign in to the dashboard + click "Authorize this CLI".
        The dashboard binds the code to your account + a fresh API
        key with the scopes you select on the confirmation screen.</li>
      <li>The CLI polls <code>POST /v1/auth/cli-authorize/exchange</code>
        and once the binding lands, stores the plaintext API key in
        the OS keyring (macOS Keychain / Linux libsecret / Windows
        Credential Manager).</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Activation codes expire in <strong>5 minutes</strong>. If you
      get an "expired" error, just re-run <code>driftstack login</code>.
      The flow is single-use — once the CLI binds + retrieves the
      key, the code is deleted so a second authorize attempt
      against the same code fails fast.
    </p>

    <h2>Common commands</h2>
    <pre><code>{`# List active sessions on your account
driftstack sessions list

# Create a new session against the default archetype
driftstack sessions create --label "qa-run-001"

# Navigate that session to a URL
driftstack sessions navigate ses_… --url https://example.com

# Snapshot the current page (returns a profile state blob)
driftstack profiles snapshot --session ses_… --label "post-login"

# Destroy a session
driftstack sessions destroy ses_…

# List your API keys
driftstack api-keys list

# Mint a new key for CI
driftstack api-keys create --name ci-bot --scopes read,write`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Profile-based config</h2>
    <p>
      The CLI supports named profiles so you can switch between
      personal + team + sandbox accounts:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`# Authorize a second profile
driftstack login --profile team-prod

# Run any command under that profile
driftstack --profile team-prod sessions list

# List configured profiles
driftstack config profiles

# Set the default profile
driftstack config use-profile team-prod`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Each profile holds its own API key in the OS keyring + its
      own base URL (so you can point at staging:
      <code>--base-url https://staging-api.driftstack.dev</code>).
    </p>

    <h2>Scripting + machine-readable output</h2>
    <p>
      Add <code>--json</code> to any command to get the raw API
      response. Errors go to stderr; exit code 0 on success, non-
      zero on any HTTP error.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`# Parse with jq
driftstack sessions list --json | jq '.data[] | .id'

# Exit-code based check
if driftstack sessions get ses_xxx --json > /dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo "session still alive"
fi`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Environment variables (CI / containers)</h2>
    <p>
      In CI you can't open a browser. Use a long-lived API key
      created in the dashboard:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`export DRIFTSTACK_API_KEY=ds_live_…
export DRIFTSTACK_BASE_URL=https://api.driftstack.dev   # optional override

driftstack sessions list`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Env vars override the OS-keyring-stored profile. If both are
      set, env wins — useful for tests that want to ignore your
      personal profile without touching its config.
    </p>

    <h2>Logout / rotate</h2>
    <pre><code>{`# Remove the current profile's key from the keyring
driftstack logout

# Logout a specific profile
driftstack logout --profile team-prod

# Rotate the API key in-place (server mints new + revokes old after grace)
driftstack api-keys rotate <key-id>`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Where the CLI stores config</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Profiles:</strong> <code>~/.config/driftstack/profiles.toml</code>
        (Linux) / <code>~/Library/Application Support/driftstack/profiles.toml</code> (macOS).</li>
      <li><strong>API keys:</strong> OS keyring under the
        <code>driftstack-cli</code> service name. Never plaintext on disk.</li>
      <li><strong>Cache:</strong>
        <code>~/.cache/driftstack/</code> — short-lived
        response caches for tab-completion etc.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt><code>driftstack: command not found</code></dt>
      <dd>Re-source your shell config after install
        (<code>source ~/.zshrc</code>), or check that
        <code>$(brew --prefix)/bin</code> is on PATH.</dd>

      <dt>"Authorization code expired" during login</dt>
      <dd>You took longer than 5 minutes to click Authorize, or
        the dashboard session timed out. Re-run
        <code>driftstack login</code>.</dd>

      <dt>"Keyring locked" on Linux</dt>
      <dd>Your <code>libsecret</code> daemon (gnome-keyring) is
        locked. Unlock with <code>gnome-keyring-daemon --replace</code>
        or set <code>DRIFTSTACK_NO_KEYRING=1</code> + use env-var
        auth instead.</dd>

      <dt>Slow on first command after install</dt>
      <dd>The Bun runtime warms its cache on first use. Subsequent
        invocations are sub-100ms.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://docs.driftstack.dev/quickstart-curl/">/docs/api-quickstart</a> —
        the REST API the CLI wraps.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/api-keys/">/docs/api-keys</a> — scope ladder
        for keys minted by <code>driftstack login</code>.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/sessions/">/docs/sessions</a> — session
        lifecycle, the meat of what the CLI orchestrates.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Support</h2>
    <p>
      CLI bugs:
      <a href="https://github.com/driftstackdev/driftstack-api/issues">github.com/driftstackdev/driftstack-api</a>.
      Auth-flow questions:
      <a href="mailto:developers@driftstack.dev">developers@driftstack.dev</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
